hat we just don't know what to do it  but in radiology and as a patient I was  perfectly comfortable that I'm a healthy  individual this clearly isn't doing  anything to me so far so it's probably  okay now this is going to become  important as we start launching some of  these big precision medicine initiatives  that you probably have been hearing  about and will continue to hear about  and a bit over a year ago President  Obama announced what we called the  precision medicine initiative or now  something that's referred to as all of  us and the reason this is so critical is  that this particular program is really  meant to build the largest database in  the United States of personal  information of the of our participants  that contributes to personalized  medicine so that's not just going to be  genetics that's going to be people's  fitbit's that's going to be their  medical records that's going to be a lot  of information about their health so  that that data can be mined by  researchers and can be used to advance  the concepts of personalized or  precision medicine  some of the things that I just mentioned  Fitbit data healthcare records genetic  data we think of that as pretty  sensitive stuff and we were really  curious from our perspective on how  likely a broad population was to accept  that so here in San Diego we partnered  with what's called the San Diego blood  bank to work on a project and a  community genomics initiative that  really brought together the altruism of  a blood bank population with the  innovation of genetic technology and so  some of you may know our San Diego  Chargers seem to have migrated a bit up  north now but historically the Chargers  have had an annual blood drive every  year right before Thanksgiving and it's  the biggest blood drive one of the  biggest blood drives in the world it's  actually one McGuiness world record for  largest blood drive or more at most  blood donated and this has really been  an institution in San Diego so we  thought it would be great to partner  genomics education at this event with  the test that actually looked at 70  blood donors to see if we could get  blood donors to contribute genetic data  what type of data they might be  interested in contributing and really if  we could even use blood centers as a  good merchant the reason this is  important is that our typical  participant in whole genome sequencing  or genomic studies right now tends to  fall into a very narrow category they  are mostly male they are mostly  Caucasian they are mostly well-educated  they're mostly wealthy and this is data  that's based on a program that are  understanding your genome participants  participate in call people seek that's  run out of dr. Robert greens greens  group at Harvard and this particular  study is looking at lots of different  initiatives that have done personal  genome sequencing like uig and this is  sort of what they're finding so this was  not all of their participants but we see  this trend pretty typically across other  studies as well so the issue here is  that the more diversity we have in  genomics the better job we're going to  do improving personalized and precision  medicine  everybody and part of the precision  medicine initiative part of their charge  is actually to get a representative  sampling of the population of the United  States and we know that this is not what  the United States looks like we need to  do a much better job of recruiting  people of different ethnicities  different education levels in order to  really hit that goal so that's what we  focused on as part of this pilot project  that we did in collaboration with the  San Diego blood bank  this particular graph and I have many  more on a lot of other characteristics  this happens to focus on race what we  did was we took the San Diego census and  we sent out invitations to a huge group  of blood donors and got more than 7,000  responses from people who were  interested in participating in this type  of study so a really really phenomenal  response rate from a very engaged  altruistic population which wasn't  surprising to us but it was what we were  hoping for um what you can see here is  that we actually do have a really great  representation of race we did the same  for education level we did the same for  income we split it up by gender by age  so we were able to really select a very  representative sampling of San Diego and  if anything we were able to over select  for populations that are typically under  represented so more women more  ethnicities that in races that turn to  be underrepresented so what did we find  we found that our concern of whether  they would provide this information or  not was fortunately probably not an  issue what they were more than more than  60 percent of people were willing to  provide information from their fit bed  information about their lifestyle  biological samples genetic data and they  were willing to connect to contribute  survey data the only things that were a  little bit lower than that was  information from their health record  which is kind of understandable there's  obviously a lot of concern about sharing  that information and information from  their social media accounts which we all  thought  sort of interesting considering how much  people put out on social media so what  we were able to show was that when we  start looking at how we can engage with  a healthy population number one is that  partnering with great organizations that  are trusted in a community is very  valuable but overall people of all  different races ages economic  backgrounds are really willing to  participate they understand genomics is  important and they want to contribute so  if we can reach them in the right ways  we have a really good shot of getting a  very successful and diverse study in the  PMI so what are some of the other things  that we learned one of our biggest  lessons and this is something that I  think is the most critical as we move  forward in implementing this in  healthcare is that we actually find a  lot in a genome that we presume to be  healthy there's a lot of people who now  will not say there's a healthy genome  and I think I would probably agree with  that because we know that all of us have  things in our family we have certain  risks for diseases and there's a lot of  different places that this can actually  be beneficial before someone gets sick  or before they start to take a  medication so this is a graph of a group  of uig participants that came from the  Mayo Clinic in Florida and what you can  see across the bottom are the numbers of  our individual participants so 1 through  39 and each of these colored bars  actually represents from one common drug  where that particular individual had a  different than typical metabolism for  that medication so the in this first  column you can see four colored blocks  that particular individual had a  different than typical metabolism for  the loop integral also known as cravaack  and for tricyclic antidepressants and  for two places that two things that  impact warfarin there's howell ism now  this is my pharmacogenetics is kind of  considered the low line these are very  very common drugs that are prescribed  now that doesn't mean that these people  were taking these medications but we  know that there are some  they plant that they may plan to take  out some time in the future and  certainly with warfarin what often  happens is they don't know they're going  to take that information what will come  is that somebody will get a blood clot  and the first medication that they are  typically given to treat that blood clot  is warfarin now we also have the ability  with the genome to help to start  figuring out who's higher risk for those  blood clots and so that was the second  thing that we looked at with these  particular individuals these purple  stars at the top of bar 5 and bar 27  those purple stars indicate people who  have a mutation called factor v leiden  and the factor 5 Leiden mutation is  something that can increase the chance  that somebody will develop a blood clot  in their lifetime it's not a huge  increase but it is somewhat significant  particularly when it's in combination  with other risk factors like birth  control pills are smoking so not only  are these two individuals people who are  more likely to get blood clots than  somebody in the general population but  they're also the people who have  different than typical metabolisms from  warfarin so these are people who would  benefit from the potentially a different  don't starting dose of warfarin to have  a lower rate of complications or in  efficacious dosing so this is one of the  big advantages of whole genome  sequencing or testing genomics and a  healthy person it's not just getting the  pharmacogenomics data it's being able to  look at other risk factors and tying  those two pieces together now it's not  just about those things and certainly  not everybody will have a response for  some of these different things but  sometimes there's actually even more  significant things that come up so in  our San Diego blood bank study one of  the really neat things that we were able  to do is to partner with a group called  inform DNA to offer a genetic counseling  and return of results to each of our  participants so we collected a really  detailed family history and we were also  able to talk a lot to them about their  results and get feedback about what  their experience was  and what they plan to do moving forward  this particular case was a young male  and when he first consented to the study  he hadn't talked about any specific  family history and he didn't report  anything's too significant she the  genetic counselor after his results were  available and when he was asked why he  decided to participate he said that the  San Diego blood bank hassles them pretty  regularly about his blood type so he  thought why not now that's obviously not  the reason that most people decide to  pursue genetic testing it doesn't tend  to be a just because I wanted she would  just because sort of thing so this is  kind of interesting because he really  wasn't expecting to find much and he  really wasn't going into the test trying  to gather information so what did we  find one of the things that we found was  a likely pathogenic variant in a gene  that we know can increase risk for  cardiomyopathy this particular gene  impacts risk for ARV C which is the  genic right ventricular cardiomyopathy  for those who may not be as familiar  this particular form of cardiomyopathy  can be related to sudden death and so  it's really important for us to be aware  of these types of risks before an  individual has a cardiac event  the other thing we found was that he's a  carrier for that common cystic fibrosis  variant but I mentioned before Delta at  5:08 so when we looked at his family  tree  he's the square down there the 26 year  old with the arrow pointing to him and  it was sort of interesting because a RVC  is actually what we call a dominant  genetic disease and that means that  oftentimes there's a family history when  we looked at his family though it  actually makes sense if you see the  brackets around his mother  so the 49 year old that means that she  was adopted so he actually did not have  any information about his mother's side  of the family and we also know that  cardiomyopathy is not fully punishment  so it is possible to have a genetic  variant that could cause the disease but  not ever develop it and if that risk for  developing disease  does go up with age so his mom's still  fairly young it may be that over time  she would have a risk to develop that  particular disease but hasn't yet so  well what did we learn from this just  this one genome sequence we've learned  that this particular individual has this  risk for cardiomyopathy himself and most  likely he inherited that from his mother  although she would need to be tested to  confirm that so if we confirm that we've  essentially been able to identify two  people that certain almost certainly  have an increased risk of cardio Mile  cardiomyopathy and as a result of sudden  cardiac death we also know that his  brother has a 50% chance of inheriting  that cardiomyopathy variance so he also  needs to be tested and we could then  identify up to three people in this  family that have that significant risk  his brother also has a 50% chance of  carrying not cystic fibrosis variant so  where that plays in is if they decide to  have children and their children also  have a chance to inherit both of those  variants and if either of them have a  child with a woman who's a carrier for  cystic fibrosis though they're children  not only could be at risk for cardiology  but also for cystic fibrosis so how is  he feeling and what is he gonna do next  um he certainly did say that the ARV C  was unexpected but when he had  originally had the consenting session  the genetic counselor who consented him  had talked to him about the fact that he  was his mother was adopted and that when  we do find surprises in the family  history or surprises in the genomic  information that are pretty big things  it tends to be because we don't know  part of the family history so that big  consenting process and really making  sure that he knew what to expect in  advance was really valuable to him and  since he had time to think about it and  know that there was something that might  come up because of his mother's unknown  family history he was doing okay with  this information he said he's going to  get a cardiologist and  did say that he would talk to his family  and he did understand that both his  mother and his brother were potentially  at risk to have this thing genetic  variant so this is really one of the  things that we recognized and of course  this is just one example and what I can  tell you though is this is not atypical  for understand your genome participants  in our 70 San Diego blood bank  participants we actually had another  gentleman who we diagnosed as having a  hereditary form of arrhythmia and not  only did he report a history of  arrhythmia but he reported that his  granddaughter a young girl in her teens  was actually recently put on exercise  restrictions and had some sort of heart  thing so you know 70 people that we  tested completely randomly from the  population in San Diego two of those 70  people had pretty important findings  that impacted not only them but their  family members and that's just one set  of examples the two genes that I  mentioned that related to the  cardiomyopathy and the arrhythmia a part  of a set of genes that we call the ACMG  56  so a few years ago the American College  of medical genetics and genomics came  out with a list of 56 genes and what  they said was that if you're going to do  whole genome or whole exome sequencing  for any reason where disease or for  healthy people you should always look  for these 56 genes and that's because  these are what we call highly actionable  genes so these are genes like brca1 and  2 that predispose for hereditary breast  and ovarian cancer the genes related to  Lynch syndrome which is a hereditary  form of colon under neutral ovarian and  other cancers and cardiomyopathies and  hereditary arrhythmias along with some  other disorders we didn't know how  common these things were going to be  when we look at them historically and  what we see in the population they don't  seem that common by themselves but what  we actually found in our lab was that  about 2 and 1/2 percent of our healthy  people  actually had known pathogenic mutations  in one of these 56 genes these are  almost all what we call dominant  diseases again genes that if you have  even one pathogenic variant you might  develop the disease and you're a  first-degree relatives so parents  siblings children also have a 50% chance  to develop those same conditions so  these are really really high impact  variants for us to find and we're  actually finding them in about one in 40  people in the general population so as  the genetic counseling that was actually  really shocking to me and it's not just  in our lab that we're finding this this  is really pretty consistent that work  that other groups are finding as well so  what is all this lead us to I think the  really exciting thing about what might  come next is that the more data we  collect in a healthy population the more  we have the ability to inform things  around population screening so back in  2014 dr. Mary Claire King who is  incredibly well known for being one of  the discoverers of the brca1 and brca2  hereditary cancer genes dr. King was  awarded the Lasker Award and as part of  the Lasker Award she published this  particular paper and really made the  case for screening everybody for brca1  and brca2 now historically we've had  very specific groups of people that we  offer this genetic testing to and a lot  of that rationale was number one it's  not likely to find a mutation if you  don't fit into one of these predefined  categories and number two if you don't  fit into one of those previously defined  pre previously defined categories the  odds of finding one of those uncertain  results exceeds the odds of finding  something significant what she says in  this paper though is that in the United  States the number of brca1 and brca2  carriers is about one in three  - one in 500 people now this particular  case she focused on women because  they're the highest risk of developing  the cancers related to these genes so  that means that in the u.s. there are  somewhere between two hundred and fifty  thousand to four hundred and fifteen  thousand adult women that have brca1 or  2 mutations and 14 breast and ovarian  cancer is both highly likely and  potentially preventable so that's a  pretty big number and what dr. King had  was able to demonstrate was that even  when we did this testing in an  unselected population these individuals  so no known family history no personal  history these women were still very high  risk for breast cancer  there were a lot a couple different  groups so HudsonAlpha  where dr. jacob is one and a lab called  council in san francisco was another  that sort of took her challenge on and  said we're gonna offer testing for these  genes to everyone so we can learn more  about what these look like in a general  population and the really interesting  thing they found was that about 50% so  half of the people who had these  pathogenic mutations in these very very  important predatory cancer genes did not  fall into one of our previously defined  groups they didn't have the personal or  family history that genetic counselors  like me or others would have used to  identify them as high-risk and they very  likely could have developed cancer  without ever knowing they were BRCA  positive so this to me was really  impactful if we're missing 50% of these  women because of our criteria then  that's pretty strong evidence to me that  we should start thinking about testing  everybody and that's dr. Chang's  recommendation her recommendation which  she's been out talking about regularly  is that every woman age 30 and older  should be offered hereditary testing for  brca1 and brca2 and that any pathogenic  result be returned to those women so  this made me start thinking about our  ACMG 56  so there are about 238 million adults in  the u.s. one in 40 people so two and a  half percent that have those ECM g56  variants that's almost 6 million adults  in the US so that's six million of us  that are walking around with genetic  variants that could cause hereditary  cancer risk hereditary cardiac disease  risk and other genetic disease in so  that was a pretty impactful number to me  as well and I think that's one of the  things that we should be thinking about  as we start to move this forward there  is a lot of potential for benefits now  again one of the arguments that's come  up around the 56 ACMG genes is that we  don't know what the penetrance is if  there's not a family history of this  these disorders so when we talk about  penetrance again with genetic diseases  we're talking about how likely it is  that if you have a pathogenic variant  you will get the disease and the  suspicion was that potentially people  who did not have a family history would  be have a variant that has lower  penetrance than people who did have a  family history just not an unreasonable  hypothesis so fortunately dr. Robert  Greene at Harvard tested this and he  actually went back and looked at two big  studies one was the Framingham Heart  Study and one was the Jackson Heart  Study so this was about 462 European  Americans and in the Framingham study  and about 3,000 african-americans in the  Jackson Heart Study  what they did was actually do whole  exome sequencing on all of the samples  from individuals in these studies and  they have a lot of history about these  individuals and their health status so  they looked at those 56 genes without  matching them to their health status of  their clinical data and look to see how  many of those individuals had mutations  in these genes and then how many had  diseases that were tied to that so what  you can see in this particular table is  that when they looked at in the  vigils who carried variants in the ACMG  genes the number of conditions that were  related to these genes far exceeded what  they observed far exceeded what would be  expected by the population risk alone  so they took cardiovascular history into  consideration certainly because these  were those heart studies but they were  hard studies of people that were  generally healthy so when they looked  for instance at the Framingham study  they found two individuals that had  mutations in hereditary cancer genes and  both of those individuals had actually  gone on to develop cancer so what this  is telling us is even when people are  not selected because of their family  history it seems that the penetrance of  these particular genes is still fairly  strong and warrants potential testing so  as we move forward into the world of  genomic testing I think there's no  question that we're going to start  seeing this more and more in healthy  individuals fortunately we're going to  take some of the uncertainty we have now  we're going to do studies like the study  that we did with the blood bank and the  study Robert Greene is doing if people  seek and then with this data around  penetrance and we're going to learn more  so that we can apply it in the best  possible way so the message that I want  to leave you with is that this is a  process and not only that but one of the  things that we need to do is to not  think of whole genome sequencing as a  test in medicine we tend to think of  tests or most of our tests that we run  are one-time events and whole genome  sequencing is really a resource it's  something that we should be able to  continue to go back to as we learn more  as our knowledge based changes and as  new things happen for the individuals  who are sequence so that we can ask  better questions and get better answers  thank you  thank you miss Ramos for that  informative presentation now before we  get started on the question and answer  session I would like to remind our  audience how to submit questions you can  submit questions by typing them in the  Q&amp;A box which can be found by clicking  on the green Q&amp;A button at the lower  left of the presentation window we'll  try to get to as many questions as we  can our first question is well what do  professional organizations say about  sequencing healthy people so currently  there are a couple different  recommendations that have come out by  societies like the National Society of  genetic counselors and the American  College of medical genomics genetics and  genomics at this point there's nothing  that says that healthy people shouldn't  consider getting in sequencing and that  really adults should decide for  themselves if getting testing is  something that they're interested in how  long on what they do recommend is that  that testing be done in the context of  genetic counseling both before the  testing and after the testing so really  having making sure those individuals are  well educated about the benefits and the  limitations of testing about any risks  that there may be for things like  insurance discrimination any concerns  that they may have about sharing that  data with family members and really if  there's anything that they may or may  not want to know so right now it's  something that isn't recommended per se  as part of normal healthcare but the  professional societies really just  suggest that if people are pursuing it  they get the right services and good  genetic counseling to go along with  next question is is this type of testing  covered by insurance so genetic testing  in general is often covered by insurance  if there's a personal or family history  of the disease that it's been that is  being looked at so for instance in cases  of whole genome sequencing or whole  exome sequencing in sick kids or sick  adults for that matter it is often  covered by insurance although the amount  of coverage may differ and that's one of  the reasons that we recommend talking to  genetic counselors to help figure out  what testing will be covered and how  much out of pocket expense patients  might have doing this type of testing  when a healthy population is not  typically covered so that's one of the  questions around the testing that  maryclaire King is promoting and one of  the concerns is that if you're a young  healthy person with no family history of  cancer your insurance isn't going to pay  for testing the advantage though is that  now that this sequencing technology is  getting less expensive and more people  are doing this type of testing and  historically there was one lab that did  BRCA testing and that testing cost about  $4,000 now there are clinical labs that  offer testing for this and other genes  at a cost of about two hundred and fifty  dollars so it's now coming to the point  where at least in some cases up hurt a  healthy person can access this type of  testing without the big out-of-pocket  expense  thank you we have time for one last  question what do you think it will be  common for everyone to get genome  sequencing as part of their health care  so I think we're still a few years off  from that and I think the estimates that  we've seen pretty frequently fall in  about the five to ten year range and  that's I don't think maybe when the  majority of people will start doing it  but certainly where it will start to be  more and more common and as importantly  we'll have doctors that are educated  about how this testing can be used will  have health care systems and technology  systems that can make it valuable and so  I think that within the next five to ten  years we'll see a lot more healthy  people with genetic information and  their records pharmacogenomic  information before they get prescribed  drugs things along those lines I'd like  to once again thank ms Erica Ramos for  her presentation ms Ramos you have any  final comments for our audience just  that I appreciate you all taking the  time to learn a little bit more about  this topic  thank you again miss Ramos I'd also like  to thank our sponsor lab roots for  making today's educational webcast  possible today's webcast will be  available for on-demand viewing through  May 22nd 2017 you will receive an email  from lab roots alerting you when this  webcast will be available for replay we  invite you to forward that announcement  to your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event thank you for joining  us then we hope to see you next time  goodbye
 

